"The model: N atoms that do not interact, in a square box with hard walls, in two dimensions so that the whole wavefunction can be drawn as a surface. The levels are E = (h²/8mL²)(nx² + ny²); each holds on average {#}/(e^((E − μ)/kT) − {#}) atoms (the Bose–Einstein distribution), and the chemical potential μ is solved so that the occupations add up to N. The condensate is the ground state nx = ny = {#}, drawn exactly: N₀ · ({#}/L²) sin²(πx/L) sin²(πy/L). The dots on the ladder are one sample of those occupations (each atom keeps its own random number, so the same atoms sink as the gas cools); the readouts give the averages.",
"The packets are a picture of the thermal atoms, not a second calculation: an atom at temperature T has a momentum spread √(mkT) along each axis, and the smallest packet consistent with that spread (the uncertainty principle) has a position spread σ = λ_T/({#}√{#}π), about a fifth of the thermal de Broglie wavelength λ_T = h/√({#}πmkT). Each excited atom is drawn as such a packet, moving at the speed of its level (ħπ√(nx² + ny²)/mL) and bouncing off the walls; its stripes in the phase view are its de Broglie wavelength. Packets smaller than a grid cell are drawn as dots. The condensate has one phase everywhere, turning at E₀/ħ, slow compared with the thermal packets. The surface height is the square root of the density, so the thin hot packets and the broad condensate fit in one picture.",
